    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-iii.jpg" alt="Ivan III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ivan_III_of_Russia_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Tsardom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a>, which under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> (r. 1462–1505) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-iii-of-moscow">Vasily III</a> (r. 1505–1533) had absorbed Novgorod, Tver, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a>, thrown off the overlordship of the Great Horde and become the dominant power among the Russian principalities. Ivan III had married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophia-palaiologina">Sophia Palaiologina</a>, niece of the last Byzantine emperor, used the double-headed eagle and occasionally styled himself tsar, and Russian churchmen developed the idea of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> as the heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, a ‘<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-rome">Third Rome</a>’. On 16 January 1547 his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a>, then sixteen, was crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> of All Russia by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macarius-metropolitan-of-moscow">Metropolitan Macarius</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dormition-cathedral-moscow">Dormition Cathedral of the Moscow Kremlin</a>. The title, derived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <em>caesar</em> and used in Russia for Byzantine emperors and Mongol khans, asserted that the ruler of Moscow owed allegiance to no one and stood equal to any emperor. The early years of Ivan’s reign brought reforms by his circle of advisers, and in 1552 his army stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>, followed by the annexation of the Khanate of Astrakhan in 1556. These conquests gave Moscow control of the entire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and made the tsardom, for the first time, a multi-ethnic state ruling large Muslim and other non-Russian populations.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/london"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="London" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:London_Skyline_(125508655).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ilya Grigorik, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Muscovite society was overwhelmingly rural, with most people peasants growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rye">rye</a>, oats and barley in a harsh northern climate with poor soils and a short growing season. Over the period the peasantry was progressively bound to the land. Peasant movement had already been restricted to a fortnight around St George’s Day in late autumn, ‘forbidden years’ suspending even that right were introduced from the 1580s, and the law code of 1649 abolished any time limit for recovering runaways, completing the legal establishment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>. Slaves (<em>kholopy</em>) formed a significant further category. Trade grew steadily: the English navigator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-chancellor">Richard Chancellor</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea">White Sea</a> in 1553, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscovy-company">Muscovy Company</a> was chartered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1555, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk">Arkhangelsk</a>, founded in 1584, became the main port for trade with western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">Furs</a>, especially sable, collected in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> as tribute (<em>yasak</em>), were among the most valuable exports and a key source of revenue. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> shaped daily life and culture, and a patriarchate was created in 1589. In the 1650s the liturgical reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patriarch-nikon">Patriarch Nikon</a> split the church: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-believers">Old Believers</a> who rejected them were condemned in 1666–1667, and their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avvakum">Avvakum</a> was burned in 1682. Urban unrest, as in the Salt Riot of 1648 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a> Riot of 1662, and the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> and peasant revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stepan-razin">Stepan Razin</a> in 1670–1671, showed deep social tensions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-molodi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-molodi.jpg" alt="Battle of Molodi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-molodi">Battle of Molodi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Molodi_-_v_pam%27at%27_pobedy_1572_goda_-_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wilder, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The army rested on the provincial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> cavalry, whose members held service estates (<em>pomestia</em>) in return for campaigning, supplemented from about 1550 by the <em>streltsy</em>, a permanent infantry corps armed with handguns, and by artillery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a>’s capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> in 1552 was followed by the long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a> of 1558–1583, which began with conquests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> but ended in defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Poland-Lithuania</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-bathory">Stephen Báthory</a> and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, with the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>’s gains. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a> burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1571, but were defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-molodi">Battle of Molodi</a> in 1572. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a> Polish forces occupied Moscow from 1610 until a national militia led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuzma-minin">Kuzma Minin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-pozharsky">Prince Dmitry Pozharsky</a> expelled them in 1612, and the peace treaties of Stolbovo (1617) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-deulino">Deulino</a> (1618) cost Russia its Baltic coast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>. From the 1630s the government raised ‘new-order’ regiments trained by foreign officers. In the Thirteen Years’ War of 1654–1667, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereiaslav</a> agreement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaporozhian-cossacks">Ukrainian Cossacks</a>, Russia regained Smolensk and won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> and left-bank <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-andrusovo">Truce of Andrusovo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter I</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azov">Azov</a> in 1696 with a newly built fleet, was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII of Sweden</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> in 1700, rebuilt his army and won decisively at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Battle of Poltava</a> in 1709, and later at sea off Hangö in 1714.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sobornoe-ulozhenie"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sobornoe-ulozhenie.jpg" alt="Sobornoe Ulozhenie" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sobornoe-ulozhenie">Sobornoe Ulozhenie</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sobornoe_Ulozhenie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The tsar was an autocrat in theory, ruling with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyar-duma">Boyar Duma</a>, a council of leading aristocrats, and in close alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a>. Government business was handled by a growing number of central chancelleries (<em>prikazy</em>), each responsible for a function or territory, staffed by professional secretaries and clerks, while provincial governors (<em>voevody</em>) ruled the towns and districts. Precedence among noble families, <em>mestnichestvo</em>, governed appointments until it was abolished in 1682. Assemblies of the land (<em>zemskie sobory</em>) met from 1549 and were at their most important in the seventeenth century, when one elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-of-russia">Michael Romanov</a> tsar in 1613 and another approved the law code of 1649, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobornoe-ulozhenie">Sobornoe Ulozhenie</a></em>, which remained the basis of Russian law until the 1830s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a>’s reign also produced the law code of 1550 and the <em>oprichnina</em> of 1565–1572, a separate realm under the tsar’s personal control in which his black-clad servants terrorised boyars, clergy and towns, culminating in the massacre at Novgorod in 1570. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">Romanov</a> tsars Michael (r. 1613–1645), Alexei (r. 1645–1676) and Feodor III (r. 1676–1682) strengthened the bureaucracy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter I</a> transformed it, creating the Senate in 1711, provinces (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">gubernii</a></em>) from 1708 and administrative colleges from 1717, and replacing the patriarchate with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-synod">Holy Synod</a> in 1721.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/red-square"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-square.jpg" alt="Red Square" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/red-square">Red Square</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kremlin_and_Red_Square.1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">mos.ru, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The tsardom turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>’s principality into the largest state in the world. After the cossack leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yermak-timofeyevich">Yermak Timofeyevich</a> attacked the Khanate of Sibir in 1582, Russian traders, cossacks and soldiers advanced across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> along its rivers, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobolsk">Tobolsk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomsk">Tomsk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yakutsk">Yakutsk</a>, and reached the Pacific at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-okhotsk">Sea of Okhotsk</a> in 1639. In 1648 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semyon-dezhnev">Semyon Dezhnev</a> is believed to have sailed through the strait between Asia and America. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nerchinsk">Treaty of Nerchinsk</a> of 1689, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>’s first treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a>, fixed a border north of the Amur, leaving the river to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In the west, Russia’s acquisition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> and left-bank <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in 1667 began the long and contested intertwining of Russian and Ukrainian history. The tsardom also created lasting institutions and symbols: the autocratic tsar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>, the service nobility and the image of Moscow as the centre of Orthodoxy. Its architecture includes St Basil’s Cathedral on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-square">Red Square</a>, built in 1555–1561 to commemorate the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>, and the tent-roofed churches and painted interiors of the seventeenth century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-fyodorov">Ivan Fyodorov</a> printed the first dated book in Moscow, the <em>Apostol</em>, in 1564. The schism with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-believers">Old Believers</a> created communities that persist today, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter I</a>’s reforms, built on seventeenth-century foundations, launched Russia as a European great power.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava.jpg" alt="Battle of Poltava" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Battle of Poltava</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marten%27s_Poltava.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pierre-Denis Martin, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The tsardom did not fall; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter I</a> transformed it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. Proclaimed co-tsar with his half-brother Ivan V in 1682 under the regency of their sister Sophia, Peter took real power in 1689. After his Grand Embassy to western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1697–1698, he crushed a revolt of the streltsy, ordered nobles to shave their beards and wear western dress, introduced a new calendar from 1700 and demanded lifelong state service from the nobility. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> drove further change. Peter founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a> in 1703, building a fleet and a new capital to which the court moved during the 1710s, and created a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscript</a> army maintained by heavy taxation. The victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Poltava</a> destroyed Swedish power in the east, though a campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> ended in near-disaster on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prut">Pruth</a> in 1711 and the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azov">Azov</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nystad">Treaty of Nystad</a>, signed on 10 September 1721, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a> and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a>, securing its Baltic coast. On 2 November 1721 (22 October Old Style) the Senate conferred on Peter the titles of Emperor of All Russia, Father of the Fatherland and ‘the Great’. The tsardom thus became the Russian Empire, which lasted until the revolution of 1917, while the title of tsar remained in everyday use.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Tsardom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> turned Muscovy into a vast multi-ethnic state stretching from the Baltic approaches to the Pacific, entrenched <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autocracy">autocracy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> identity, survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">Romanovs</a>, and was transformed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in 1721.</em></p>

